[Letter]: {T / Y / P / E / any letter}
[Spatial Action]: {fold / curve / intersect / layer / twist}
[Paper Color]: {ivory / muted blue / sage green / warm sand}
[Accent Color]: {burgundy / cobalt blue / olive / ochre}
[Title]: {e.g. P / CURVE}
[Format]: 9:16 vertical

Create an experimental editorial typography poster where a large, clearly recognizable letter is physically formed from real sheets of matte paper.

Keep about 70–80% of the letter structure recognizable, then transform it through [Spatial Action]. Let the paper fold, curve, overlap, intersect, or move through different depths while preserving the essential anatomy of the letter.

Use thin, flexible paper rather than thick 3D blocks. Show realistic paper edges, subtle surface texture, natural bends, soft tension, and convincing deformation.

Use [Paper Color] as the main paper tone with [Accent Color] appearing only where the structure changes — inside a fold, curve, intersection, cut edge, or deeper layer. Keep the palette muted and editorial, not brightly saturated.

Use strong directional studio lighting to create large graphic shadows across the paper and background. Let light and shadow become part of the composition while keeping the paper form readable.

Keep typography restrained. Use one medium-size title such as “[Title]”, one small series label, and only a minimal amount of micro text. Use modern Grotesk sans-serif typography with lighter weights, generous spacing, and soft charcoal or gray ink rather than heavy pure black.

Leave large areas of paper completely free of text so the material, color, form, and shadow remain the main visual focus.

Build clear foreground, middle ground, and background relationships. Allow parts of the paper letter to extend beyond the frame for scale and tension.

Overall aesthetic: experimental typography, Swiss editorial design, paper sculpture, modern graphic design, restrained color, high-end art direction, realistic studio photography.

A town built from glossy interlocking plastic toy bricks on a huge green baseplate, square 1:1, in the spirit of a classic seek-and-find book: a real physical model photographed in sharp focus from an extremely high and wide oblique angle so that the whole town fits in one picture, everything is tiny and there is an enormous amount to look at. The town is divided into clear districts by long brick stairways, terrace walls, a tram line, a river and streets. Top left: a hill with a grey brick castle with towers and plain pennants, a pine forest and a winding path. Top right: an observatory with a dome, a small rocket launch pad with a rocket and a tall crane. Top centre: a clock tower on a hill with a long staircase down to the town. Centre: a big town square on a wide stepped terrace with a fountain of clear blue bricks, a carousel with plain bench seats, market stalls with striped awnings (fruit, flowers, ice cream, bread, toys, books), cafe tables with umbrellas, a bandstand and a parade. Left and right of the square: tightly packed colourful townhouses with red, blue, yellow and white roofs, balconies with flower boxes, narrow streets, stairs and a tram passing through. On the right: a train station with a platform and a small train. Lower right: a toy shop, a cafe and a row of small shops. The lower third of the picture is only the waterfront: a blue river of translucent bricks with small boats, a lift bridge, a promenade with trees, a lighthouse on a pier, cargo cranes, crates and strolling people. About 180 minuscule block figures, each doing something: shoppers, stall keepers, a marching band, cafe guests, kids on the stairs, passengers at the station, tram riders, people fishing from the pier, boat crews, workers on the crane. A standing person is only about 3% of the picture height.

The look is the point: glossy ABS plastic bricks with round studs on every flat top, tiny seams between bricks, slight reflections, tiles, plates, slopes and transparent pieces; people are tiny block figures with cylindrical yellow heads with simple printed faces and small round hands. Bright daylight, a clear sky with white brick clouds, saturated primary colours, sharp focus everywhere (no blur, no tilt-shift). Not a flat digital vector, not a 3D render, not a painting. The picture fills the whole frame to all four edges: no table edge, no box, no border, no caption. No brand names or brand logos.

Exactly one small logo: a small flat dark navy printed tile above the door of the toy shop in the lower right of the town, about 4% of the picture width, with a cream-coloured cat paw print whose big pad holds a swirling ocean wave, and the word "Shoji" in cream cursive brush lettering to its right. Apart from that tile there are no letters, words, numbers or digits anywhere: no shop signs with writing, no station boards, no timetables, no posters; the clock face has plain tick marks only.

THE ONLY ANIMALS IN THE WHOLE PICTURE ARE FOUR CATS, and there are NEVER MORE THAN FOUR: not five, not a spare one, no look-alike twin of any cat. No other animal at all: no toy animal bricks, horses, dogs, birds, fish, dinosaurs, dragons, dolphins, butterflies or insects; no animal-shaped boats, signs, statues, fountains, flags or mascots; nobody wears an animal head, animal ears, a tail or cat ears. The four cats are tiny cats like the people, all at the same distance from the viewer, each in a DIFFERENT kind of place at a DIFFERENT height: their feet are between about 15% and 60% of the picture height measured from the top, and NONE is in the lower third. Each cat is in the open, never hidden behind anything, and each appears only ONCE. The three real cats are each TINY: only about 0.7% of the picture height (about 14 pixels in a 2048-pixel-high picture), about a quarter of the height of a standing person, a bare cat with only a thin red collar with a tiny gold bell. From left to right:
1. Shoji Cat, an upright standing orange tabby cat about three heads tall in a black leather jacket, denim shorts, cream sneakers and black wayfarer sunglasses with blue and red lenses, dancing with one arm raised on a small round stage at the far left of the picture, among a few people, feet at about 38% of the height, about one and a half times as tall as each of the other three cats;
2. ONE brown mackerel tabby (warm brown fur with dark stripes, white muzzle and chest) sitting upright in the open cargo bed of a parked green delivery truck in the middle of the picture, just right of the fountain, feet at about 58%. This is the only tabby;
3. ONE calico cat (white with orange and black patches) sitting upright on the ridge of a red roof of a townhouse in the upper right, beside a chimney, feet at about 18%. This is the only calico;
4. ONE black-and-white "hachiware" cat (black head and back, a white blaze splitting the face, white chest and paws) sitting upright on the top step of the stairs of the station building at the right edge, feet at about 46%. This is the only hachiware cat.
No black or dark round objects, no piles of dark pieces, and no dark animal-like shapes that could be mistaken for a cat. No watermark.

A hand-painted watercolor picture-book illustration of a steep Mediterranean-style hill town running down to a small harbor, square 1:1, in the spirit of a classic seek-and-find book: seen from an extremely high and wide oblique angle so that the whole town fits in one picture, everything is tiny and there is an enormous amount to look at. The town is divided into clear districts by long stairways, terrace walls and a winding road. Top left: an orchard and vineyard terrace with a round stone tower, a small white chapel and a courtyard with washing lines. Top right: a school with a courtyard and a bell tower, a small hilltop park with a pavilion. Centre: a big market square on a wide stepped terrace with a fountain, many striped-awning market stalls (fruit, bread, flowers, pottery, cloth), cafe tables with parasols, and a church with a clock tower. Left and right of the square: terracotta-roofed houses packed up the hillside with flowerpot balconies, hanging laundry, green shutters, lemon trees, narrow lanes and steep stairs with people on them. Lower right: a bakery and a row of small shops. The lower third of the picture is the harbor: a stone quay, a promenade with trees, boat sheds, fishing boats and sailboats on blue water, a lighthouse on a small breakwater, nets and crates on the quay, strolling people and a seaside garden. About 180 minuscule painted people, each doing something: shoppers and stall keepers, cafe guests, children on the stairs, a baker, a priest at the church door, schoolchildren, people hanging laundry, painters at easels, sailors on the quay, strollers. A standing person is only about 3% of the picture height.

The look is the point: a real hand-painted watercolor picture-book illustration with fine dark-brown pen-and-ink outlines, transparent watercolor washes with soft wet-in-wet blooms, cold-press paper grain, and a warm sunny palette of terracotta, ochre, sage green, sky blue and rose. Every person and cat has clean ink outlines so it stays readable. Not a flat digital vector, not a 3D render, not a photograph. The picture fills the whole frame to all four edges: no paper border, no frame, no caption.

Exactly one small logo: a small dark navy hand-painted signboard above the door of the bakery in the lower right of the town, about 4% of the picture width, with a cream-coloured cat paw print whose big pad holds a swirling ocean wave, and the word "Shoji" in cream cursive brush lettering to its right. Apart from that board there are no letters, words, numbers or digits anywhere: no shop signs, no street names, no menus, no price tags, no banners, no boat names; the clock face has plain tick marks only.

THE ONLY ANIMALS IN THE WHOLE PICTURE ARE FOUR CATS, and there are never more than four. Not five, not a spare one, and no look-alike twin of any cat. No other animal at all: no dogs, donkeys, horses, goats, chickens, doves, gulls or any bird, no fish, no animal statues, signs, awning prints or weather vanes. No person wears an animal head, animal ears or costume. The four cats are tiny painted figures like the people, all in the upper and middle part of the picture: their feet are between about 15% and 60% of the picture height measured from the top, and NONE is in the lower third (the lower third is only harbor, quay, promenade, boats, lighthouse, trees and the seaside garden). Each cat is in the open, never hidden behind anything. The three real cats are each TINY: only about 0.7% of the picture height (about 14 pixels in a 2048-pixel-high picture), about a quarter of the height of a standing person, and wear a thin red collar with a tiny gold bell:
1. a black-and-white "hachiware" cat (black head and back, a white blaze splitting the face, white chest and paws) sitting upright on a stone step of a stairway beside a basket of bread, in the left half;
2. a brown mackerel tabby (warm brown fur with dark stripes, white muzzle and chest) sitting upright on a flat garden wall beside a row of flowerpots, left of centre;
3. a calico cat (white with orange and black patches) sitting upright under the red-and-white striped awning of a fruit stall in the market square among fruit crates, right of centre;
4. Shoji Cat, an upright standing orange tabby cat about three heads tall in a black leather jacket, denim shorts, cream sneakers and black wayfarer sunglasses with blue and red lenses, dancing with one arm raised in the crowd of the market square, in the middle of the picture, about one and a half times as tall as each of the other three cats.
No black or dark round objects, no piles of black cloth, and no dark animal-like shapes that could be mistaken for a cat. No watermark.

